A.cards("c7s1",[{"id": "g7-1-standard", "h": "7.1 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-1-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.1"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mountain source to stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A sedimentary environment is a place where material is produced, moved or deposited. Steep mountain slopes release large angular blocks.</li><li>Mountain streams can carry coarse sediment through bedrock canyons, and channels may split and rejoin as braided streams beyond the mountains. Fluvial describes river processes and deposits; it does not name one grain size.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Meanders and deltas</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On gentler terrain, a meandering stream is bordered by a floodplain. Floodwater spreads mud and sand across that surface.</li><li>At a river mouth, the current enters standing lake or ocean water and slows. Sediment accumulates into a delta that builds outward. A floodplain lies alongside a channel; a delta grows at its mouth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Wind and ice select differently</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wind transports sand and finer particles. Finer material can travel onward, leaving dunes composed mainly of well-sorted sand.</li><li>A glacier instead carries a wide mixture, from ground rock flour to large blocks. Melting releases both water and sediment. Different agents can therefore produce quite different deposits from the same mountain source.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lakes and wetlands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A lake contains several environments, from wave-worked shorelines to quieter central water. The shoreline may be sandy or rocky depending on the material available. In wetlands, saturated ground supports abundant vegetation.</li><li>Water can limit exposure to oxygen, helping plant material become an important part of the accumulating sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret a collection of clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Topography, water and local geology control which environments develop. To interpret an ancient deposit, combine grain size, sorting, structures and biological remains.</li><li>Angular blocks beside a cliff support a slope setting; rounded gravel in a channel supports stream transport. One sand layer alone cannot distinguish a desert, river or beach.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A river slows on entering a lake → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Delta</mark>.</li><li>Fine material spreads beyond a flooded channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Floodplain</mark>.</li><li>Wind concentrates similar sand sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sand dune</mark>.</li><li>Moving ice releases a broad sediment mixture → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Glacial deposit</mark>.</li><li>Waterlogged ground preserves plants → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Wetland</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 1\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p1 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
