A.cards("c7s3",[{"id": "g7-3-standard", "h": "7.3 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-3-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.3"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Break the rock physically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Physical weathering breaks rock apart without requiring a change in its minerals. Near-surface fracturing can accompany unloading or a fall from a cliff.</li><li>Freezing water and growing salt crystals widen cracks.</li><li>Roots can pry fractures apart, while burrowing exposes material.</li><li>Thermal expansion can contribute, but this textbook explicitly qualifies its relative importance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change the minerals chemically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chemical weathering changes or removes minerals.</li><li>Dissolution puts soluble components into water. Oxidation forms oxides, including reddish iron oxides. Hydrolysis reacts water with silicate minerals and commonly produces clay plus dissolved material.</li><li>Acids and other biological reactions can also break minerals down. These are chemical changes, not simply the rounding of a stone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Different minerals leave different products</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Starting composition matters. Soluble limestone can develop pits and grooves as water removes material.</li><li>In granite, feldspar may alter to clay or survive as grains, resistant quartz commonly remains as sand, and dark minerals can yield clay and iron oxides.</li><li>One original rock can therefore supply several sediment sizes and compositions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fractures expose new surfaces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fracturing makes a rock weaker and increases the surface available to water, air and organisms.</li><li>Physical breakup can accelerate chemical reactions, and chemical alteration can weaken rock for further breakup. Highly fractured parts of a unit may erode into notches or lower terrain.</li><li>The two forms of weathering often reinforce each other.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport changes the fragments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>After weathering loosens material, streams, waves, wind, ice and gravity can move it.</li><li>Collisions break clasts, and abrasion scrapes surfaces and rounds exposed corners. Moving sediment can also scour bedrock and create more fragments.</li><li>Keep the steps distinct: weathering produces loose or dissolved material, erosion removes it, and transport carries it elsewhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Water freezes and widens a crack → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Physical weathering</mark>.</li><li>Feldspar reacts to form clay → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hydrolysis</mark>.</li><li>Mineral components enter solution → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dissolution</mark>.</li><li>Iron-bearing material reacts with oxygen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Oxidation</mark>.</li><li>Moving grains scrape a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Abrasion</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
