A.mc("e2-c7-standard-3-check","c7-3-core","A fractured granite sheds quartz grains while feldspar changes to clay. Which description separates the mechanisms?",["*Physical breakup releases quartz; hydrolysis alters feldspar|Fracturing can free unchanged grains while hydrolysis chemically alters feldspar.", "Chemical dissolution releases quartz; compaction makes clay|Quartz grains are shed, not stated to dissolve; compaction does not chemically make clay.", "Abrasion releases quartz; cementation alters feldspar|Fracture is the stated grain-release process; cementation binds grains rather than causing this alteration.", "Hydrolysis releases quartz; physical breakup makes clay|This reverses the chemical alteration and mechanical grain-release mechanisms."],"Fracturing can free unchanged grains while hydrolysis chemically alters feldspar.",["TB7:7.3"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S3"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.3 · PDF p6 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-3-write","c7-3-core","Name the four chemical-weathering mechanisms listed in this section, with a brief process description for each.","Dissolution puts soluble components into water. Oxidation combines susceptible material with oxygen. Hydrolysis reacts water with minerals and commonly produces clay. Biological reactions, including plant acids and bacterial activity, chemically break minerals down.",["Names and describes dissolution.", "Names and describes oxidation.", "Names and describes hydrolysis.", "Names and describes biological chemical reactions."],["TB7:7.3"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S3"], "focusedWriting": true});
