A.cards("c7s4",[{"id": "g7-4-standard", "h": "7.4 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-4-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.4"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Name the particle sizes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Gravel includes granules, pebbles, cobbles and boulders. Sand is finer than gravel, while silt and clay together make mud.</li><li>The supplied size table gives the exact boundaries. Clay is both a size term and a mineral-group name, so a clay-sized particle and a clay mineral describe different properties.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Roundness concerns edges</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular clasts have sharp edges and corners. Repeated collisions and abrasion tend to remove those exposed corners, producing smaller, rounder pieces.</li><li>Shape is distinct from size: a large cobble can be rounded, and a tiny sand grain can remain angular. Rounding does not by itself specify one transport distance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sorting concerns the size range</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Poorly sorted sediment mixes many sizes. Moderately sorted sediment has a more limited range, and well-sorted sediment concentrates similar sizes.</li><li>Wind-built dunes commonly contain well-sorted sand because wind cannot supply gravel and finer particles may move onward. A mixture can be rounded and still poorly sorted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow transport from the source</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Near a bedrock source, sediment commonly starts coarse, angular and poorly sorted. Transport can break it, round it and separate sizes.</li><li>Farther along a stream or repeatedly worked by waves, finer and better-sorted material may remain. These are useful tendencies whose interpretation still depends on source rocks and available sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Check four transport controls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The textbook identifies four controls: slope steepness, current strength, sediment supply and the transporting agent. Strong turbulent water can move larger clasts than weaker flow, but it cannot carry a size the source does not provide.</li><li>Glaciers can move large mixtures, while wind is selective. Describe size, roundness and sorting before interpreting the setting.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Range of particle sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sorting</mark>.</li><li>Sharpness of edges and corners → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Roundness</mark>.</li><li>Slope, current, supply and agent → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Controls on transport</mark>.</li><li>Silt and clay together → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mud</mark>.</li><li>Loose rock fragments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Clastic or detrital sediment</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
