A.cards("c7s6",[{"id": "g7-6-standard", "h": "7.6 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-6-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.6"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Precipitate dissolved material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Nonclastic sediment can form from dissolved components instead of fragments broken from older rock. As salty lake or sea water evaporates, minerals such as halite and gypsum can precipitate.</li><li>Other chemical reactions also make minerals. Evaporation removes water; the dissolved material is left behind and becomes concentrated before precipitation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Construct carbonate with chemistry and life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Calcium-rich spring water can deposit layered calcite as travertine. Organisms also extract dissolved chemicals to build shells and skeletons.</li><li>Accumulated carbonate remains can become limestone. Chemical and biological processes may work together, so the contrast concerns the stated formation pathway rather than an absolute separation of all natural examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish chalk and dolostone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chalk is a soft, very fine-grained limestone made from microscopic calcium-carbonate remains.</li><li>Dolostone can form when magnesium-bearing groundwater reacts with limestone, replacing calcite with dolomite. This changes the mineral composition of an existing carbonate rock; it is different from simply compacting an untouched pile of shell fragments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Silica and iron make other rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chert is silica rich. It can form from accumulated microscopic silica shells or from chemical precipitation, including nodules in carbonate rock.</li><li>Iron formation contains thin layers rich in iron minerals, commonly with quartz, and much of it formed early in Earth history. Similar layering therefore does not mean identical composition or origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Preserve plants as peat and coal</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wetlands can accumulate plant debris faster than it is completely destroyed. Peat retains much of the original plant texture and pore space.</li><li>With burial, compaction and heating, peat loses water and oxygen and becomes coal. Carbon-rich coal records plant material; it is not an evaporite and is not made from a pile of quartz sand.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Halite and gypsum left as water evaporates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Evaporite deposits</mark>.</li><li>Layered calcite precipitated around a spring → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Travertine</mark>.</li><li>Microscopic carbonate remains accumulate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chalk</mark>.</li><li>Magnesium-bearing fluid replaces limestone minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dolostone</mark>.</li><li>Silica-rich shells accumulate or silica precipitates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chert</mark>.</li><li>Plant debris is buried, compacted and heated → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Coal</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
