A.mc("e2-c7-standard-6-check","c7-6-core","Two fine-grained rocks form from microscopic shells: one is carbonate, the other silica rich. Which pair fits?",["*Chalk and chert, respectively|Microscopic carbonate remains make chalk; silica-rich remains can make chert.", "Chert and chalk, respectively|This reverses the two source-supported compositions.", "Coal and halite, respectively|Coal is plant derived and halite is an evaporite.", "Gypsum and coal, respectively|Gypsum is an evaporite and coal comes from plant material."],"Microscopic carbonate remains make chalk; silica-rich remains can make chert.",["TB7:7.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S6"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im4.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.6 · PDF p6 · unchanged source geometry."}, {"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im2.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence B from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure B · Exploring Geology 7.6 · PDF p6 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-6-write","c7-6-core","Explain two different ways chert can form. State the composition shared by both pathways.","Silica-rich microscopic shells can accumulate and later become chert. Silica can also precipitate chemically, including nodules where waters interact in carbonate rock. Both pathways produce a silica-rich rock.",["Describes accumulation of silica-rich remains.", "Describes subsequent lithification for that pathway.", "Describes chemical precipitation, including a supported example.", "Identifies silica-rich composition in both cases."],["TB7:7.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S6"], "focusedWriting": true});
