A.cards("c7s7",[{"id": "g7-7-standard", "h": "7.7 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-7-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.7"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate formation, bed and contact</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A distinctive laterally traceable rock unit can be called a formation. A formation may contain many individual beds.</li><li>Bedding can be defined by grain size, composition or color, and the boundary between rock units is a contact. Contacts may be sharp, gradational or scoured; a sharp boundary alone does not establish its cause.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change conditions between beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Layers can record a discrete flood, changes in current strength or direction, a new sediment supply, changing sea level or changing climate.</li><li>Each explanation needs the actual evidence. Different colors do not automatically mean different climates, and a thick bed does not supply a universal amount of elapsed time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Settle a normally graded bed</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shake mixed sediment in water and let it settle. Coarser particles settle first, with progressively finer material above.</li><li>A normally graded bed therefore becomes finer upward as transport energy wanes or suspended sediment settles. Read the variation within one bed. Water left above a jar deposit is not another sedimentary layer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Move a dune and retain cross beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A current moves grains over a dune or ripple crest. Grains accumulate on its down-current slope, and migrating dunes preserve inclined layers called cross beds.</li><li>Cross beds can form in water as well as in wind. Large dune-scale sets and other clues support a desert interpretation; inclined layers alone do not prove one.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare complete structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Parallel beds commonly form as layers accumulate on one another. A scoured contact instead records erosion before the next deposit.</li><li>Keep parallel bedding, cross bedding and grading distinct: they concern layer orientation, inclined internal deposits and an upward grain-size change. Compare the actual source photograph before identifying the process.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Distinctive laterally traceable rock unit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Formation</mark>.</li><li>Boundary between rock units → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact</mark>.</li><li>Coarse base grades to finer sediment above → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normally graded bed</mark>.</li><li>Inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Layers accumulate with similar orientations → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Parallel beds</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
A.match("e2-c7-standard-7-bank","c7-7-core","Match each source-supported observation or process to its interpretation. A term may be reused if appropriate.",[["Distinctive laterally traceable rock unit", "Formation"], ["Boundary between rock units", "Contact"], ["Coarse base grades to finer sediment above", "Normally graded bed"], ["Inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes", "Cross beds"], ["Layers accumulate with similar orientations", "Parallel beds"]],"Use the full observations. Parallel beds commonly form as layers accumulate on one another.",["TB7:7.7"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S7"], "wordBank": true});
A.mc("e2-c7-standard-7-check","c7-7-core","One bed has a coarse base and fine top. Another preserves inclined layers inside an otherwise layered sandstone. Which pair fits?",["*Normal grading, then cross bedding|Upward fining is grading; inclined internal depositional layers are cross beds.", "Cross bedding, then normal grading|This reverses the two diagnostic observations.", "Normal grading, then a scoured contact|The second observation is internal inclined bedding, not an erosional boundary.", "A scoured contact, then cross bedding|The first observation is a size trend within a bed, not erosion at its boundary."],"Upward fining is grading; inclined internal depositional layers are cross beds.",["TB7:7.7"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S7"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.7 · PDF p8 · unchanged source geometry."}, {"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence B from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure B · Exploring Geology 7.7 · PDF p9 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-7-write","c7-7-core","Contrast normal grading with cross bedding. Explain one process producing each.","A normally graded bed becomes finer upward as coarse material settles first during waning flow or settling. Cross bedding consists of inclined layers left as grains accumulate on a migrating dune or ripple’s down-current side.",["Describes the upward-fining trend.", "Explains coarse-first settling or waning flow.", "Describes inclined internal layers.", "Explains deposition on a migrating dune or ripple."],["TB7:7.7"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S7"], "focusedWriting": true});
