A.mc("e2-c7-standard-8-check","c7-8-core","Beach cobbles are well rounded but remain near their bedrock source. Which explanation fits?",["*Repeated wave abrasion with little net movement|Waves can pound stones near their source and round the edges.", "Long river transport is required for every rounded cobble|Repeated local wave reworking supplies another pathway to rounding.", "Chemical alteration alone establishes the roundness|The observed energetic wave setting provides a physical abrasion mechanism; roundness alone does not establish one chemical cause.", "Uniform clast size establishes a long transport distance|Sorting and net distance are separate questions, and the stem supplies no size uniformity."],"Waves can pound stones near their source and round the edges.",["TB7:7.8"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S8"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p10-Im3.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.8 · PDF p10 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-8-write","c7-8-core","Explain why rounded conglomerate does not establish one exact transport distance. Include one stream example and one beach example.","A stream rounds clasts during collisions and abrasion as it transports them. Waves can repeatedly pound beach stones near their source, also rounding them. Shape records reworking, but different pathways can produce similar rounding over different net distances.",["Connects rounding with abrasion.", "Explains a stream transport pathway.", "Explains repeated beach reworking with limited net movement.", "Rejects one exact distance from rounding alone."],["TB7:7.8"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S8"], "focusedWriting": true});
