A.mc("e2-c7-standard-11-check","c7-11-core","Groundwater enlarges cracks in limestone, and a subsurface opening eventually collapses. Which sequence fits?",["*Dissolution forms a cave; roof collapse forms a sinkhole|Groundwater removes carbonate, and a cave roof can later collapse.", "Roof collapse forms a cave; cementation forms a sinkhole|The stated opening develops through dissolution; mineral binding does not explain the later surface collapse.", "Compaction forms a cave; dissolution raises the surface|Packing does not excavate the dissolved opening, and removal does not raise the surface.", "Cementation forms a cave; weathering fills the opening|Cement adds mineral material; the stem describes removal and subsequent roof failure."],"Groundwater removes carbonate, and a cave roof can later collapse.",["TB7:7.11"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S11"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p9-Im2.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.11 · PDF p9 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-11-write","c7-11-core","Explain how limestone can both form a resistant cliff and develop a cave or sinkhole. Distinguish dissolution from collapse.","In some dry settings limestone resists erosion and forms cliffs. Groundwater in wetter settings can dissolve carbonate along fractures and bedding, leaving caves. A sinkhole can form when a cave roof collapses. Rock resistance depends on conditions and structure.",["Gives the dry-setting resistance condition.", "Explains dissolution removes carbonate.", "Locates cave development along water-accessible pathways.", "Distinguishes roof collapse and sinkhole formation from dissolution."],["TB7:7.11"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S11"], "focusedWriting": true});
