A.cards("c7s12",[{"id": "g7-12-standard", "h": "7.12 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-12-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.12"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance the sea across land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied model, the sea is on the left and land is on the right. A transgression moves the shoreline landward as relative sea level rises, whether the water rises or the land subsides.</li><li>Beach sand covers an earlier erosion surface. Follow one fixed location while neighboring environments move across it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stack sand, mud and reef carbonate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At the fixed location, an early beach is followed by a lagoon and then an offshore reef. The preserved order is beach sandstone, lagoon mudstone and reef limestone, from oldest at the bottom to youngest at the top.</li><li>Different environments also coexist laterally. Their sedimentary expressions are called facies.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retreat the sea and build outward</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During regression, the shoreline and neighboring facies shift seaward, toward the left in this source. Lagoon mud spreads over earlier reef limestone, and beach or dune sand advances over mud.</li><li>Previously deposited material can become exposed and reworked. New layers accumulate above the older transgressive package rather than replacing its entire history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare two vertical successions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example transgression records sandstone below mudstone below limestone. Its regressive succession reverses the environmental trend: limestone, then mudstone, then sandstone.</li><li>Those particular rocks belong to this illustrated setting, not every coastline. Direction of shoreline migration and independent environmental clues carry the interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Let a layer end naturally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A layer need not continue forever. A facies can thin out where its depositional environment ends, and a channel deposit may be restricted to the channel itself.</li><li>Erosion can also remove material. Before correlating two exposures, consider both lateral facies differences and later removal rather than assuming a missing bed was never deposited anywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shoreline and facies migrate landward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression</mark>.</li><li>Shoreline and facies migrate seaward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regression</mark>.</li><li>Different sedimentary expressions of neighboring environments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies</mark>.</li><li>Environment ends and its deposit thins away → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies pinchout</mark>.</li><li>Gravel stays within a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Restricted channel deposit</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
