L.CH7_STANDARD.lessons.push({"n": 13, "bank": [["Wet sediment contracts during drying", "Mudcracks"], ["Inclined deposits slope down-current", "Cross beds"], ["Flat clasts lean against flow", "Imbrication"], ["Back-and-forth waves make balanced ridges", "Symmetric ripples"], ["Identify actual grains, structures and fossils first", "Observation before interpretation"]]});
A.cards("c7s13",[{"id": "g7-13-standard", "h": "7.13 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-13-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.13"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe before interpreting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Begin with observations: color, clast size, roundness, sorting, bed thickness and contacts. Red colors commonly reflect oxidation, while dark gray may accompany organic material and low oxygen.</li><li>These clues support interpretations when combined. Color alone does not uniquely identify land, water depth or one depositional environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read processes in the structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Grading can record weakening flow or settling. Large cross beds in well-sorted sand support wind-built dunes.</li><li>Mudcracks form when wet mud contracts during drying and may later fill with sand. A mudcracked bed records exposure to drying, but identifying the exact coastal or inland setting still needs context.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use fossils and flow indicators</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Marine fossils support seawater deposition, while plant remains help interpret land and climate context. In the supplied examples, cross beds slope down-current, while imbricated river clasts lean against the current.</li><li>Asymmetric ripples can record one flow direction; symmetric ripples commonly reflect back-and-forth waves. Keep the indicators and their directions distinct.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the example from the bottom</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example begins with well-sorted, cross-bedded dune sandstone, then shell-bearing beach sand, then marine shale and reef limestone. This upward passage records a transgression.</li><li>Younger sand with land plants and coal records a later retreat of the sea. The interpretation follows the changing evidence at one location.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Finish with a new source of debris</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular granite boulders in muddy breccia cap that example. Limited rounding and nearby granite terrain support debris flows from steep mountains.</li><li>This is a later tectonic and sediment-supply episode. Preserve the distinction between the observed fragments and the inferred setting, and do not transfer this five-unit example onto the separate four-unit plateau investigation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wet sediment contracts during drying → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mudcracks</mark>.</li><li>Inclined deposits slope down-current → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Flat clasts lean against flow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Imbrication</mark>.</li><li>Back-and-forth waves make balanced ridges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Symmetric ripples</mark>.</li><li>Identify actual grains, structures and fossils first → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Observation before interpretation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
