A.mc("e2-c7-standard-15-check","c7-15-core","A Dakota sandstone ridge stands above shale valleys west of Denver. What directly explains the relief?",["*Resistant tilted sandstone outlasts weaker shale|Differential erosion leaves sandstone hogbacks above shale valleys.", "Modern ridge height directly records greater deposition depth|Present topographic relief does not directly measure ancient water depth.", "Shale deposition necessarily postdates modern ridge erosion|The sedimentary units existed before their later deformation and erosion.", "Map color indicates stronger cement in every green unit|Map colors encode geological units and ages; they are not direct tests of cement strength."],"Differential erosion leaves sandstone hogbacks above shale valleys.",["TB7:7.15"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S15"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p3-Im2.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.15 · PDF p3 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-15-write","c7-15-core","Use two examples west of Denver to distinguish the original depositional environment from later deformation and erosion.","Dakota sandstone records shoreline and beach deposition; later tilting and differential erosion produced its hogback. Morrison stream, wetland and lake deposits record earlier sedimentation; their dinosaur tracks were made before the beds were tilted.",["Identifies Dakota shoreline or beach deposition.", "Explains later tilting and resistant-ridge erosion.", "Identifies a supported Morrison environment.", "Separates original tracks/deposition from later bed orientation."],["TB7:7.15"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S15"], "focusedWriting": true});
