A.cards("c7s16",[{"id": "g7-16-standard", "h": "7.16 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c7-16-core"], "src": ["TB7:7.16"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate landscape observation from history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau photograph shows cliffs, ledges and softer slopes. These are modern expressions of differing rock resistance.</li><li>The gray slope and tan cliffs help locate units, but exact depositional settings require the supplied column, textures and fossils. The investigation has four numbered units, with unit four oldest at the bottom and unit one youngest at the top.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Start with the oldest river deposits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit four includes rounded pebbles, coarse sand, wood, plant-bearing mudstone and dinosaur tracks. Together they support a river system and floodplain.</li><li>This is not the dune sandstone at the base of a different Connect sequence. Always identify which source column is being interpreted before transferring an answer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance from shoreline sand into marine shale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit three has yellow-tan quartz sandstone with marine shell fragments. Its basal conglomerate cuts an erosion surface and locally includes wood and leaves, supporting beach and river influence.</li><li>Overlying unit two is organic-rich gray shale with marine fossils and local limestone. This change from land through shoreline toward marine mud supports a transgression.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Return toward shore and coastal land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Upper unit one starts with shell-bearing tan sandstone and passes into sandstone, mudstone, plants, mudcracks and coal. Shoreline or delta deposition gave way to coastal land and wetlands.</li><li>That upward change above marine shale supports regression. The upper package is sandstone and mudstone with coal, not a reef limestone cap.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">State the complete history and its evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>From oldest to youngest, the sequence records river and floodplain, shoreline sand, marine mud, then shoreline and coastal land. The whole record is a transgression followed by a regression.</li><li>The lower yellow-tan sandstone is compared with the Dakota in the Denver example. Give observations for each interpretation rather than treating a matching name as independent proof.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit 4: rounded gravel, wood, plants and dinosaur tracks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">River system and floodplain</mark>.</li><li>Unit 3: shell-bearing sand with basal river evidence → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach and river influence</mark>.</li><li>Unit 2: organic gray shale with marine fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Muddy marine water</mark>.</li><li>Unit 1: sand, mud, plants, mudcracks and coal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shoreline to coastal land</mark>.</li><li>Land → marine mud → coastal land → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression followed by regression</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the specific observations and source figure. A rock name or color alone rarely establishes one unique environment."], "media": []}]);
A.match("e2-c7-standard-16-bank","c7-16-core","Match each source-supported observation or process to its interpretation. A term may be reused if appropriate.",[["Unit 4: rounded gravel, wood, plants and dinosaur tracks", "River system and floodplain"], ["Unit 3: shell-bearing sand with basal river evidence", "Beach and river influence"], ["Unit 2: organic gray shale with marine fossils", "Muddy marine water"], ["Unit 1: sand, mud, plants, mudcracks and coal", "Shoreline to coastal land"], ["Land → marine mud → coastal land", "Transgression followed by regression"]],"Use the full observations. From oldest to youngest, the sequence records river and floodplain, shoreline sand, marine mud, then shoreline and coastal land.",["TB7:7.16"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S16"], "wordBank": true});
A.mc("e2-c7-standard-16-check","c7-16-core","The plateau passes upward from river deposits through shell-bearing sand into marine shale, then coal-bearing coastal beds. Which history fits?",["*Transgression followed by regression|Land-to-marine change records sea advance; return toward coastal land records retreat.", "Regression followed by transgression|This reverses the observed environmental trend.", "Transgression with no later retreat toward land settings|Plants, mudcracks and coal above marine shale support the later return to coastal land.", "Regression throughout the entire four-unit sequence|The earlier change from river deposits toward marine shale records transgression, not continuous regression."],"Land-to-marine change records sea advance; return toward coastal land records retreat.",["TB7:7.16"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S16"], "media": [{"kind": "img", "src": "assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png", "alt": "Photographic or diagram evidence A from the course source; inspect the visible properties.", "cap": "Figure A · Exploring Geology 7.16 · PDF p4 · unchanged source geometry."}], "sourceFigure": true});
A.teach("e2-c7-standard-16-write","c7-16-core","Summarize the plateau’s four units from oldest to youngest. Use two observations to justify the whole-sequence transgression and regression.","From unit 4 to 1: river/floodplain; beach and river influence; marine mud; shoreline and coastal land. Basal gravel with wood and tracks supports land, while shale with marine fossils supports sea advance. Upper plants, mudcracks and coal above marine shale support retreat.",["Orders units 4 → 3 → 2 → 1.", "Gives the four supported environmental groups.", "Uses basal land and overlying marine evidence for transgression.", "Uses upper coastal-land evidence for regression."],["TB7:7.16"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C7S16"], "focusedWriting": true});
